## F-REVIEW-001 - a reviewer reports its own truncation boundary as a defect

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** An automated reviewer reports that a file is truncated,
  malformed, or missing its closing block - at exactly the point where the diff
  it was given stops. The file on disk is complete and parses.
- **Root cause:** The reviewer's input is a windowed or truncated diff, not the
  file. It has no way to distinguish "the content ends here" from "my view of
  the content ends here", so it describes the edge of its own window as a
  property of the code.
- **Check:** Before accepting any "file is truncated / unterminated / missing"
  finding, parse the file on disk and say so in the reply. Make the refutation
  mechanical rather than a matter of opinion: a check that parses every
  shipped script or source file turns this class into a passing gate instead of
  a recurring argument.
- **Command:** `<PARSE_ALL_SOURCES_COMMAND>` (e.g. a test that compiles or
  parses every file of the affected type and fails if any does not).
- **Note:** taught by the reference deployment, where the same false
  "truncated file" finding was refuted twice across consecutive pull requests
  before the parse gate was added.

