## F-DERIVED-001 - derived shared state committed by concurrent changes

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** Every concurrent change conflicts on the same generated file - a
  manifest, a board or index, a lock file, a compacted registry. Rebases pile up
  on work that does not overlap in source at all, and eventually a conflict
  marker survives a resolution and reaches the mainline inside a file that is
  supposed to be machine-written.
- **Root cause:** A derived artifact is committed by every change that touches
  anything it summarises, so N parallel changes are N writers to one file. The
  artifact has no owner, and merging it by hand is guesswork because its content
  is a function of state nobody is looking at during the merge.
- **Check:** Changes ship **per-change fragments with distinct filenames**
  instead of editing the shared artifact; **one automated job on the mainline is
  the only writer** of the derived file, folding fragments in after the merge.
  Where a shared artifact must be committed directly, resolve conflicts by
  re-running its generator, never by choosing a side.
- **Command:** `<REGENERATE_DERIVED_ARTIFACT_COMMAND>`
- **Note:** measured in the reference deployment as roughly a dozen rebases and
  two corrupted generated files in a single day, before the fragment pattern.

