## F-ONESHOT-001 - a one-time script stamps success on a skip path

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** A migration or one-time fix is recorded as applied on every
  machine and never runs again - and the thing it was supposed to change is
  unchanged everywhere.
- **Root cause:** The runner marks the script done on exit code 0, and the
  script returned 0 from a path where it did no work: a dependency was absent, a
  precondition was not met yet, or a failure was caught and logged. "Nothing
  went wrong" and "the work completed" became the same exit code.
- **Check:** Every path where the work did not complete exits **non-zero**;
  enumerate the script's exit paths and assert each one in a test. Verify
  idempotency explicitly before trusting a re-run - "safe to run twice" is a
  property, not an assumption. A runner that stamps a marker reads the exit code
  as a claim about the WORK, so the script must only make that claim when true.
- **Command:** `<ONE_TIME_SCRIPT_EXIT_PATH_TEST>`

---

## F-PROBE-CACHE-001 - a boot-time race becomes a permanent silent degrade

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** A dependent feature is dead for the entire run, with one early
  log line and no further complaint. Restarting fixes it, sometimes.
- **Root cause:** A dependency was probed once during startup - while that
  dependency was itself still warming up - and the failed result was cached for
  the process lifetime. A transient condition was recorded as a permanent fact,
  and nothing ever asks again.
- **Check:** A failed probe **re-probes on an interval**; sticky failure is
  allowed only with a written justification naming why recovery is impossible.
  On recovery, validate the dependency's shape before trusting it, so a
  half-initialised service cannot corrupt state that a clean failure would have
  protected.
- **Command:** `<PROBE_RECOVERY_TEST_COMMAND>`
- **Note:** the sibling of F-STATUS-001: there the failure is reported as
  health, here it is reported once and then forgotten.

---

## F-GATE-ABSENT-001 - a merge gate satisfied by absence

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** A change merges without the gate that was supposed to stop it.
  Afterwards the check either is not there at all, or is sitting at
  `in_progress` on the commit that merged.
- **Root cause:** The gate asked "are there runs?" rather than "did the required
  check conclude successfully?". Any of three states satisfied it: a check still
  running, a check queued, and **no checks at all** - the last one arriving when
  an upstream event was dropped and nothing was ever triggered.
- **Check:** Merge only on the required check's **name plus
  `conclusion == success`**. `in_progress`, `queued`, `cancelled` and **zero
  runs** are each a distinct non-pass and none of them is a pass. Read the check
  runs themselves; a summary line that says green is a claim about a claim.
- **Command:** `<READ_REQUIRED_CHECK_CONCLUSION_COMMAND>`

---

## F-OBS-COUPLED-001 - the observability channel depends on what it observes

- **Discovered:** <YYYY-MM-DD>, <PR_OR_ITEM>
- **Symptom:** The status feed, dashboard or report goes quiet exactly when the
  observed system fails, so the worst outage produces the least evidence - and
  the silence is indistinguishable from a calm period.
- **Root cause:** The reporter runs as part of, behind, or conditional on the
  health of the thing it reports on: it starts only if the service started, or
  it collects by calling an endpoint of the dying service and gives up when the
  call fails.
- **Check:** The reporter runs **unconditionally**, on its own schedule, from
  outside the subject's lifecycle, and publishes "could not reach the subject"
  as **content** rather than by staying silent. An unreachable subject is a
  reportable state, not a reason to skip the report.
- **Command:** `<REPORTER_RUNS_WITH_SUBJECT_DOWN_TEST>`
- **Note:** the sibling of F-SCHED-001 - both turn absence of signal into
  absence of problem, one by a dead timer and one by a dead subject.

