## Inherited classes - paid for elsewhere, not yet bitten here

The entries above are the seeded set: classes that shipped and failed, in the
format every new entry follows. The four below arrive from the wider corpus and
have **not** happened in this repository yet, which is why they sit apart
rather than being filed as history that never occurred. They carry the same
fields and the same obligations.

**On first occurrence here, an inherited class is promoted**: it moves up into
the seeded set with its real `Discovered` line, and on its second occurrence it
enters the recurring-classes digest like any other. Until then, treat them as
what they are - checks worth running against a diff, written by somebody else's
outage.

### F-NATIVE-WHEEL-001 - an unpinned transitive dependency ships a bad native artifact

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** The process dies with **no stack trace in the host language** -
  no exception, no error line, often no log at all. It happens on first real
  use rather than at import, so every install check and every fast test passed
  first. Evidence exists only in the operating system's crash record.
- **Root cause:** A transitive dependency nobody selected floated onto a new
  major carrying a broken native artifact for this platform. The dependency was
  unpinned because it was never chosen, and the failure mode is invisible to
  the language's own error handling.
- **Check:** Treat a transitive dependency that ships native code as a **direct
  major** when it moves majors. Arm a native-crash handler to a file at
  start-up so the dead process names its killer. **A crash that survives a
  forced reinstall of the suspect package is a bad artifact, not a corrupt
  install**: pin below the offending major, record the pin with its lifting
  condition, file the upstream issue. Any dependency change gets the cold soak
  (`docs/UPGRADE_DISCIPLINE.md`) - the real application running long enough to
  leave the import path, because import-succeeds is not the bar.
- **Command:** <DEPENDENCY_TREE_AND_PIN_CHECK_COMMAND>

> class-guard: <assertion that no transitive native dependency is unpinned
> across a major boundary>
> VARIANT: a dependency whose native artifact loads fine and fails only on a
> second platform - the class is "native failure outside the language's error
> handling", not "the crash we saw".

### F-FLOOR-001 - a floor is a guard with an expiry date

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** A check that passed for months stops meaning anything. Nothing
  turns red: the assertion is still true and has simply stopped being
  informative, so a regression walks straight through it.
- **Root cause:** The guard was written as `>= N` over a collection that grows.
  The day the population passed `N` the check became unconditionally true. The
  same shape covers any bound the data grows past - a cap sized for today's
  volume, a timeout tuned on an empty dataset, a sample size that was most of
  the set when it was chosen.
- **Check:** A bound over a growing collection is expressed **against the
  population**, not as a constant: assert the relationship (every member, the
  exact count, the delta against a recorded baseline) rather than a floor
  somebody picked. Where a literal is genuinely right, it carries the reason it
  cannot drift.
- **Command:** <GREP_FOR_NUMERIC_BOUNDS_OVER_COLLECTIONS>

> class-guard: <assertion that no guard over a growing collection uses a bare
> numeric floor>
> VARIANT: a percentage threshold that becomes unreachable as the denominator
> grows - the shape is a constant compared against something that moves, in
> either direction.

### F-SUBSTR-001 - a substring where an identity is required

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** A check that looks right accepts something it should refuse, or
  a lookup matches a neighbour. It is most often found when a name gains a
  suffix, a host gains a subdomain, or a path gains a sibling with a longer
  name.
- **Root cause:** An identity - a host, a path, a name, an id - was matched
  with `includes` / `startswith` / `endswith` instead of being parsed and
  compared. Substring containment is not equality and does not respect the
  boundaries of the thing it is matching, so it is correct for the examples
  somebody tried and wrong for the family.
- **Check:** Parse, then compare. A host is compared as a parsed host, a path
  as a resolved path, an id as an id. Where a prefix match is genuinely
  intended, it is written with the separator included and fixtured with the
  nearest wrong neighbour.
- **Command:** <GREP_FOR_SUBSTRING_MATCHES_ON_IDENTIFIERS>

> class-guard: <assertion that identity comparisons parse rather than contain>
> VARIANT: a case-folded or unicode-normalised form that compares equal to a
> different identity - the class is "compared by a weaker relation than
> identity", not "used includes()".

### F-PARTIAL-VIEW-001 - a gate verified against less than the real gate sees

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** A check passes locally, the change is pushed on that evidence,
  and the same check fails in CI on the same commit. Nothing changed in
  between. The local run saw only part of what CI inspects. Measured in the
  kit: a secret-scanner suppression was written from a shallow clone. It named
  the one commit that clone held, and CI's full-history scan went red on the
  commit that first introduced the (fake) fixture key.
- **Root cause:** The local run checked a **subset** of CI's input and reported
  "clean" instead of "clean over what I could see". A shallow clone, a
  path-scoped run, a changed-files-only lint and a filtered test selection all
  do this. Anything derived from that run inherits the gap, such as a
  suppression fingerprint, a baseline or a "fixed" claim.
- **Check:** Before a local result stands in for CI's, name what CI inspects and
  confirm the local run saw all of it. A tool that can only see part of its
  input says so, and in CI mode that is **unknown, not clean**: it fails
  instead of passing on what it saw. Anything recorded from a scan (a
  suppression, a baseline, a fingerprint) is taken from the full view.
- **Command:** `bash tools/quality/scan.sh ci` (refuses a shallow history);
  `git fetch --unshallow` before recording a gitleaks fingerprint

> class-guard: `test_ci_scan_refuses_a_shallow_history` -- the scan lane in
> CI mode exits non-zero on a shallow clone, and passes on the full one
> VARIANT: a pre-push gate that lints only the outgoing diff while CI lints the
> tree; a rule "fixed" in one file while the same shape stands in a file the
> run never opened. The class is "the verdict covered less than the gate",
> not "shallow git".

### F-REAP-001 - not-serving read as dead, and cleanup deleting state it does not own

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** A supervisor restarts something that was alive, or two copies of
  a single-instance process run at once, or a lock file vanishes and a second
  worker starts against state the first is still writing.
- **Root cause:** Two halves of the same mistake. A supervisor treated **not
  serving** as **dead** - a port that did not answer during start-up, a health
  check that timed out - without positive liveness evidence. And a failure or
  cleanup path deleted lifecycle state (a lock, a pid file, a claim) that it
  could not prove it owned, on the assumption that a stale one was the only
  explanation.
- **Check:** Liveness is **positive evidence** (the process exists, the claim
  is held, the heartbeat is fresh), never the absence of a response. A cleanup
  path deletes only state it can prove it wrote - an owner token, a pid it
  started, a claim it holds - and otherwise reports and stands down. An
  always-on process stands down under a foreground lock rather than competing
  for the resource.
- **Command:** <SUPERVISOR_LIVENESS_AND_OWNERSHIP_TEST>

> class-guard: <assertion that no cleanup path unlinks lifecycle state without
> an ownership check>
> VARIANT: a cleanup that runs at start-up rather than on failure - the class
> is "deleting state whose owner was assumed", not "the reaper".

### F-LAUNDER-001 - a defensive default launders "could not read" into "read it, and it was empty"

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** A surface renders a healthy empty - zero findings, an empty
  list, a quiet panel - over data that was never successfully read. Found
  late, because nothing is red; the strongest form gives *advice* computed
  from a value it never read.
- **Root cause:** The idiom `x if isinstance(x, list) else []` (or
  `Array.isArray(x) ? x : []`, `or {}`, `or 0`) at a read site. It is
  contagious inside hardening passes because it wears the same shape as the
  correct checks beside it - one sibling deployment logged eleven instances,
  five written *after* the rule against it existed - and one laundering step
  **upstream** of a chain of honest per-field guards defeats all of them at
  once.
- **Check:** For every defensive default: what will the empty value MEAN to
  the caller, and can the caller tell it from a real empty result? Fix where
  the payload ENTERS, not field by field. Distinguish absent from unreadable
  using distinctions the upstream itself encodes (a serializer that emits
  null for nil); an unparseable value is NAMED, never blanked. Grade
  laundering that feeds a recommendation path above laundering that feeds a
  status - advice gets acted on.
- **Command:** <GREP_FOR_DEFENSIVE_DEFAULT_IDIOMS_AT_READ_SITES>

> class-guard: <assertion that read-path type guards raise or mark unknown,
> never silently default>
> VARIANT: F-STATUS-001 is this class observed at the render layer; this
> entry is the read-site source of it, one hop upstream.

### F-ALARM-ENTAIL-001 - a true observation alarmed as its unproven consequence

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** An alarm or finding text asserts a specific harm ("your
  traffic is exposed right now") when what was measured is a state from
  which that harm does not follow ("the tunnel is down") - while the probe
  that would actually establish the harm sits unconsulted beside it. Four
  instances landed on one surface in the field before the class was named.
- **Root cause:** Severity and the alarmed condition are different axes. The
  renderer graded by severity, which passes every unit test and still
  mislabels a legitimately-high finding as the specific consequence it
  definitionally is not.
- **Check:** For every alarm text: name the measurement, and verify it
  ENTAILS the sentence. The companion rule: an alert channel is armed by
  measured precision, not by feature completion - paging on findings the
  system is not yet confident in trains the owner to ignore the channel,
  which is worse than no alarm; the arming condition is recorded in the
  owner-decisions register with its lifting condition.
- **Command:** <SWEEP_ALARM_TEXTS_AGAINST_THEIR_MEASUREMENTS>

> class-guard: <assertion that each alarm template cites the measurement it
> renders>
> VARIANT: a report sentence that upgrades "not verified" to "verified" in
> prose - same class, the harm being a claim rather than a page.

### F-IDENT-REP-001 - identity encoded as whatever representation the first producer used

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** Two records about one referent score as two referents (a
  hostname and its IP; an id and its alias), under-counting corroboration -
  or, in the unrecoverable direction, records about *different* referents
  merge and manufacture corroboration nobody supplied. A comment beside one
  call site documents the gap for months without becoming a defect.
- **Root cause:** "These refer to the same thing" was implemented as
  equality of current representations. Nothing states what identity IS, so
  tests derived from the implementation keep passing as new producers
  arrive with different representations of the same referent.
- **Check:** Write the identity invariant explicitly, apart from any
  producer. Merging under uncertainty is asymmetric: failing to merge two
  records about one referent under-counts (recoverable); merging two
  referents manufactures evidence (not walkable back) - so ambiguous
  evidence keeps its own identity, and absent or stale evidence is unknown,
  never a silent no-match. A documented workaround beside a call site is a
  requirement nobody filed; file it, and re-ask when the next producer
  arrives.
- **Command:** <TEST_IDENTITY_JOIN_WITH_TWO_REPRESENTATIONS_OF_ONE_REFERENT>

> class-guard: <assertion that the identity join resolves representations
> before comparing>
> VARIANT: F-SUBSTR-001 is comparison by a weaker relation than identity;
> this class is comparison by an accidental one.

### F-PRECOND-001 - "not finished" treated as "not allowed"

- **Discovered:** inherited, not yet observed here
- **Symptom:** A startup gate holds users or callers out for minutes while
  something that never refuses anything warms up - a cache fill or index
  build read as a precondition, measured in the field at ~170 seconds of
  lockout per launch.
- **Root cause:** Startup *preconditions* (a check that answered NO refuses
  entry) and *progress* (a warm-up that has refused nothing and must never
  gate) were conflated into one list, so completion of work gated access.
- **Check:** Two lists, explicitly: preconditions gate, progress only
  reports. Derive the gating set from the same list the status surface
  renders, so a newly added check cannot silently start gating - or
  silently stop.
- **Command:** <ASSERT_WARMUP_STATES_NEVER_APPEAR_IN_THE_GATE_SET>

> class-guard: <assertion that the gate set is derived and names only
> refusal-capable checks>
> VARIANT: the reverse failure - a genuine precondition demoted to progress
> so the surface opens broken - is the same conflation, walked the other
> way.
